            <h2>FAQ</h2>
            <table class="faq-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Question</th>
                        <th>Answer</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>What is the normal working pressure of an aerosol can?</td>
                        <td>Typical working pressure for tinplate cans is about 8–10 bar at 55°C, while aluminium
                            monobloc cans range around 12–15 bar. Exact values depend on can design and propellant type.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Why is burst testing required?</td>
                        <td>Burst testing verifies that a can can withstand extreme internal pressure beyond operating
                            conditions. It confirms structural integrity and compliance with DOT and UN requirements.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>What temperature is used for aerosol hot-water bath testing?</td>
                        <td>Most aerosol hot-water bath tests are performed at 50–55°C to simulate elevated temperature
                            exposure during transport or storage.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>How does propellant choice affect safety?</td>
                        <td>Each propellant has its own vapor pressure curve. LPG, DME, CO₂, and HFC-152a all behave
                            differently with temperature changes, influencing can selection and fill ratios.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Can non-certified aerosol cans be used for local markets?</td>
                        <td>Using uncertified cans increases safety risks and restricts access to export markets.
                            Certified DOT/UN-compliant cans are strongly recommended.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

